\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s1\fi-360\li360\sb240\sl26\slmult0\qj\kerning28\b\caps 1.0\tab Purpose\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\kerning0\b0\caps0 Purpose of this document is to provide for calibration program of Environmental Services Pakistan monitoring and testing equipment, and serve as guideline and framework for action.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s1\fi-360\li360\sb240\sl26\slmult0\qj\kerning28\b\caps 2.0\tab RESPONSIBILITY \par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\kerning0\caps0 2.1.\tab Chief Analyst / QA Manager (MR)\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Management Representative is responsible to ensure that this program is understood and followed correctly.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 2.2.\tab QA Officers\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 QA Officers are responsible to oversee all operational aspects of this program.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 2.3.\tab Maintenance Personnel\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Maintenance personnel are responsible to carry out all repair and maintenance activities as per approved plan and procedures and to ensure that all requirements of this document are met before commissioning/re-commissioning of any equipment.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 2.4.\tab Equipment Custodians (Senior Analysts)\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Equipment custodians are overall responsible to ensure that every piece of equipment in their custody is maintained in acceptable level of calibration at all times during its service life and out of specifications equipment is clearly marked as such.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s1\fi-360\li360\sb240\sl26\slmult0\qj\kerning28\b\caps 3.0\tab Definitions\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\kerning0\caps0 Calibration \endash  \b0 The process of comparing measurement and test equipment to standards of known accuracy to detect and/or rectify deviations.\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b Out-of-Calibration \endash  \b0 The state of measurement or test equipment that is either Out-Of-Tolerance (OOT) (see below), or that has exceeded its calibration interval.\par
\b Out-Of-Tolerance (OOT) \endash\b0  The state of measurement or test equipment that fails to meet its specified level of calibration (calibrated range and/or calibrated tolerance). Note that this level of calibration may differ from the manufacturer\rquote s calibration. Failure to meet the manufacturer\rquote s calibration may therefore not create an OOT.\par
\b Range - \b0 The smallest and the largest values associated with operation of an instrument.\par
\b Manufacturer\rquote s Range:\b0  The range that is specified by the manufacturer of the unit at the time of sale, over which the Manufacturer\rquote s Tolerance (see below) applies.\par
\b Calibration Range:\b0  The range over which the unit is calibrated, which may differ, by being reduced, from the range over which the unit will operate. The calibration range is set by the equipment owner and Quality Assurance Officer keeping in mind the intended process where the device will be used.\par
\b Span \endash\b0  The number of units between the low and high value of the range. \par
\b Tolerance -\b0  The allowable deviation from a known measurement value, based on the accuracy of the unit under test. This is also known as the statement of accuracy.\par
\b Manufacturer\rquote s Tolerance:\b0  The statement of accuracy that is guaranteed by the manufacturer of the unit at the time of sale.\par
\b Calibration Tolerance:\b0  The statement of accuracy that is applied to the unit by the equipment owner and the Quality Engineer. This number reflects the accuracy established for calibration. The calibration tolerance may be wider than the manufacturer\rquote s tolerance. The value may never be tighter than the manufacturer\rquote s tolerance.\par
\b Process Tolerance:\b0  This number reflects the accuracy required for the process in which the unit operates. The process tolerance is typically wider than the manufacturer\rquote s tolerance and is often wider than the calibration tolerance. The value may never be tighter than the manufacturer\rquote s tolerance. Exceeding the process tolerance has the potential to impact the process.\par
\b Single-point Calibration\b0   When measurement and test equipment have been compared to primary or secondary standards to detect and/or adjust deviations to within the stated specifications and tolerances at one specific point. The point at which the device has been calibrated must be the point at which the device will be used. Measurements taken with the device are valid only if they are within \'b15% of the calibrated point.\par
 \b Interval:\b0  The period between calibrations of measurement and test equipment.\par
\b Measurement and Test Equipment:\b0  Instruments or devices used to measure, gauge, test, inspect, or otherwise examine.\par
\b Primary Measurement Standard:\b0  An instrument or device of known accuracy which is used in a calibration system as a standard of reference and is directly traceable to the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) or equivalent institution.\par
\b Secondary Measurement Standard:\b0  An instrument or device of known accuracy which is used in a calibration system as a standard of reference and is traceable through other standards to the NIST or equivalent institution.\par
\b Intrinsic Standard:\b0  A standard of reference by whose construction causes it to follow natural and physical laws of nature. The accuracy of the standard is controlled by the natural and physical laws of nature. Examples include the freezing and boiling points of purified water at standard sea level pressure.\par
\b Accuracy of Scales and Balances:\b0  Calculate the overall accuracy for a scale or balance, unless specified by the manufacturer, using the NIST Handbook 44, 2000, Section 2.20, Scales, T.N.3. Tolerance Values, Table 6, Maintenance Tolerances, Table 3.\par
\b As Found:\b0  The condition of an instrument/equipment prior to having an adjustment made to it. This also pertains to the data taken from this instrument/equipment.\par
\b As Left:\b0  The final condition or condition of an instrument/equipment after having an adjustment made to it. This also pertains to the data taken from this instrument/ equipment.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s1\fi-360\li360\sb240\sl26\slmult0\qj\kerning28\b\caps 4.0\tab Maintenance of Calibration Program\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\kerning0\caps0 4.1.\tab Entering Equipment into Calibration Program\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Every piece of equipment taken into service from purchase or otherwise is taken into calibration plan and equipment calibration is verified prior to its approval for use as part of commissioning qualification.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.2.\tab Maintaining a Calibrated Status\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Every month QA Officer checks the calibration plan and ensures that requirement for calibration is forwarded to calibration service provider for equipment/apparatus to be externally calibrated or assignment is made to an in-house technician/analyst to calibrate internally calibrated items.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.3.\tab Exceedance of Calibration Interval\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 In the event that an instrument has exceeded its calibration interval, the instrument is now \ldblquote Out of Calibration\rdblquote . Once notified of or detecting the condition, equipment owners are responsible for placarding the instrument as \ldblquote Out-of-Service\rdblquote  and handling these units to QA officer who will verify that required placarding is in place.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li792\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.4.\tab Changing the Calibration Status\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 The Calibration Status may be changed for equipment based on usage and criticality/process tolerance.\par
Change in the level of calibration which includes changing the calibrated range or tolerance will be considered based on system use and criticality/process tolerance.\par
Changing the status to \ldblquote Inactive\rdblquote  should be considered if the equipment will not be used for a long period as in the case of campaign specific equipment. This will reserve program resources although the possibility of failure on as-found data since the last usage and calibration must be considered. The unit must be tagged as \ldblquote out of service\rdblquote  Removing calibration stickers from inactive equipment is recommended but not required. \par
The status will be changed to \ldblquote Retired\rdblquote  for equipment no longer in service. This would typically occur for components that are damaged beyond repair, totally removed from service. Placarding of this status is not required as the component or equipment affected is no longer present in the facility or has been changed to a \ldblquote For Reference Only\rdblquote  status.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.5.\tab Performing Calibration (In House)\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 The Instrument technician calibrates the instrument using approved SOP for calibration following guidance in Standard methods.\par
When performing a calibration, the instrument should be adjusted whenever the as-found value excessively approaches the calibration tolerance. Failure to readjust the instrument will reduce the safety margin against drift during the next calibration interval. As a guide, the as-left value is recommended to provide for a safety margin \f2\u8805?\f0\lang1033 30% of the calibration tolerance. For example, if the tolerance is \'b110 units, the as left should not exceed \'b17 units when possible via adjustment. (If consistent tolerance cannot be achieved over the range of the equipment, target the operating range for the lowest deviation.) In the case that the instrument is not adjustable, resort to correction factors.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.6.\tab Performing Calibration (External Vendor)\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Either the unit is shipped to the vendor site or the vendor comes on-site depending on the agreement to complete the calibration.\par
The vendor\rquote s Calibration Certificate is scanned and attached to the file in the calibration folder and any pertinent information entered.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.7.\tab Calibration Specifications\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Calibrations require as-found and as-left data to be recorded. The only exception to this is in the case of new equipment or disposable equipment such as timers. If new equipment comes with a Calibration Certificate that has been approved by QA Manager that data may suffice as \ldblquote as-found.\rdblquote  Otherwise, if no as-found data is available, the unit is considered Out-of- Tolerance (OOT)\par
There must be at least three measurements taken (preferably five), covering at least 70% calibration range of the device or as otherwise specified.\par
The preferred calibration points are 10% and 90% of established calibration range and normal setpoint (mid span if no normal or operational setpoint).\par
The standard used to calibrate must have an accuracy better than the accepted accuracy of the unit to be calibrated.\par
Standards are NIST traceable whenever available.\par
The calibration due date for the equipment is the last day of the month of the established interval. For example, if the calibration date was 6/23/20, and the interval for that instrument is 1 year (12 months), then the calibration due date will belisted as 6/30/20.\par
The calibration attaches the generated calibration stickers. Stickers must be placed in clear view on the instrument. When calibration is performed, the old sticker must be removed and discarded before attaching the new sticker.\par
If review of the calibration record finds an error that impacts the calibration sticker, a new sticker will need to be generated once the record is corrected. If the range or tolerance was modified the sticker will need to be generated manually as those values cannot be changed on an open work record.\par
For on demand calibrations, the due date is not displayed on the sticker. A supplemental sticker to display the due date shall be placed adjacent to the calibration sticker or the date added with permanent ink and initial and dated.\par
Due to vendor variability, stickers from external vendors will vary with the format and type of information included.\par
Stickers must contain at a minimum ID of the equipment and the calibration date.\par
If the vendor sticker lacks required information a supplemental calibration sticker may be applied.\par

\pard\keepn\nowidctlpar\s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sl312\slmult1\qj\b 4.8.\tab Unscheduled Recalibration\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl312\slmult1\qj\b0 Calibrated equipment will be assessed for recalibration after repair, damage, or physical, electronic, or other changes that could impact operation, range, accuracy, or tolerance.\par
Any repair that causes the instrument case to be opened may be reason for recalibration of the equipment.\par
Any repair that deals with that section of the instrument that affects the parameters being calibrated will be reason for recalibration.\par
The calibration certificate will indicate that the reason for calibration was unscheduled and why. The comments shall state whether the unit was repaired or changed.\par

